Durarara!! AU Chapter 1 – Part 1 (All characters aged up to 18–19. Raira Academy is now a college.)
Curtis Nightstar stepped out of the station exit and into the thick, humid air of Ikebukuro like a man who had finally stopped holding his breath.
Six-foot-three of dark muscle wrapped in a heavy charcoal-gray hoodie, black face mask pulled high, black sunglasses, and a black Jumpman cap tugged low. The red of the city lights slid across the lenses every time he turned his head. First Nations Canadian, Northwest Territories born and raised, he still carried the quiet weight of long winters and open land even while standing in the middle of one of Tokyo’s busiest wards. The oversized hoodie made his frame look a little broader than it was—solid, dense, somewhere between Simon’s mountain and Shizuo’s lean violence. Not the biggest thing on the street. Just the one that didn’t look like it needed to prove anything.
He walked without a destination, hands in the kangaroo pocket, eyes moving. Neon. Cigarette smoke. The low rumble of trains overhead. The smell of grilled meat and wet asphalt after a light rain that had already dried. Everything was loud and fast and alive in a way the Territories never were. Curtis liked it immediately.
He turned a corner near a cluster of small restaurants and nearly walked into a wall of a man.
Seven-foot-three. Black. Built like someone had taken a heavyweight boxer and stretched him until the proportions became almost cartoonish. The man was holding a stack of flyers in one massive hand and thrusting them at passersby with the enthusiasm of someone who had never once been told “no” successfully. His Japanese was… ambitious. Loud, heavily accented, and missing several important particles.
Curtis stopped. Tilted his head slightly.
That guy’s tall. But his Japanese is pretty bad.
He didn’t feel the usual flicker of caution most people would. Size was just size. He’d grown up around men who could snap small trees. This one just happened to be louder about it.
Curtis stepped closer, voice low and calm behind the mask, the Russian rolling out easy and natural.
“Hello. I’m new here. Could I please have one of those flyers?”
The giant froze mid-offer. The flyers paused in the air. Dark eyes dropped, then climbed the full length of Curtis—hoodie, mask, sunglasses, the quiet way he stood like he belonged on any street he decided to stand on. For a second the big man’s expression shifted into something closer to curiosity than the usual automatic friendliness.
Then the grin split wide.
Simon’s answer came in thick, booming Russian, the kind that turned heads half a block away even when he wasn’t trying.
“New here? Ha! Then you came to the right place, little brother! Russia Sushi! Best sushi in Ikebukuro, maybe all of Tokyo! I am Simon. You take flyer, you come eat, I make sure they treat you good. You speak Russian? Good! Most people here only scream when I talk. You? You talk back. I like that.”
He shoved a flyer into Curtis’s hand with the careful force of a man who had learned (mostly) not to accidentally break smaller people’s wrists. The paper was slightly crumpled from the sheer size of the grip.
Simon leaned down a little, still grinning like the city itself was a joke only he was in on.
“You look like you can eat. Come later. Tell them Simon sent you. I will make sure they give you the big portion.”
Curtis glanced down at the flyer, then back up at the walking mountain. Behind the sunglasses his expression stayed unreadable, but the corner of his mouth under the mask might have twitched.
“I’ll keep it in mind,” he answered in the same language, voice still even.
Simon laughed—loud, open, completely unbothered by the looks people were throwing them—and clapped a hand the size of a dinner plate against his own chest.
“Good! Good! Welcome to Ikebukuro, new guy. Try not to get hit by trucks. Or by blondes with short tempers. Both are common here.”
He turned back to the next group of pedestrians, already launching into another round of broken Japanese and flyer distribution like the conversation had simply been a pleasant interruption in his day.
Curtis stood there a second longer, flyer in hand, the red of the city lights reflecting off his lenses. Then he tucked the paper into his pocket, adjusted the strap of the bag over his shoulder, and kept walking.
First night in Japan.
Already interesting.
Curtis lifted one hand from the pocket of the hoodie and tapped two fingers lightly against the black fabric covering the lower half of his face. The motion was small, almost casual.
Still speaking Russian, voice low and even behind the mask:
“Do I have to take this off when I come in?”
Simon’s eyes flicked to the mask, then to the sunglasses, then back to Curtis’s face like he was trying to figure out if the question was serious. For a second the big man just stared. Then that wide, booming laugh rolled out of him again, loud enough that a couple walking past jumped.
“The mask?” Simon waved one massive hand like he was shooing away a fly. “No, no, no. Keep it. Keep the glasses. Keep the hat. Keep whatever you want. Russia Sushi is not a place that cares about faces. We care about food and people who can eat. You come in looking like a secret agent or a bank robber, I don’t care. As long as you sit down and order something, you are welcome.”
He leaned in a little closer, still grinning, and lowered his voice to what was probably meant to be a confidential volume but still carried halfway down the street.
“Besides… a lot of people in this city wear masks these days. Some because of germs. Some because they don’t want to be recognized. Some because their face is just… like that. You keep yours. Nobody will bother you. And if anyone does—” Simon flexed one absurdly large arm once, casual and terrifying at the same time “—they will talk to me first.”
He straightened back up, shoved another flyer into the hands of a passing salaryman without looking, and turned his attention fully back to Curtis.
“You come tonight. Or tomorrow. Whenever. You keep the mask. I will still make sure they give you the big portion.”
Curtis gave a small nod, the black fabric of the mask shifting slightly with the movement. He didn’t smile—couldn’t really, behind the covering—but something in the set of his shoulders eased by a fraction.
“Understood,” he answered quietly in Russian.
Simon beamed like he’d just made a new friend for life and went back to shouting broken Japanese at the next group of pedestrians, already forgetting (or pretending to forget) that most of them had no idea what he was saying.
Curtis tucked his hand back into the hoodie pocket, flyer still safe inside, and started walking again. The red city lights slid across his sunglasses as he moved deeper into Ikebukuro.
First night.
And the giant already didn’t care what his face looked like.
That was fine by him.